#FINANCE MANAGER
## Software Requirements Specification (SRS)

**Document:** Day 1 — Requirements Specification  
**Version:** 1.0  
**Status:** Initial requirements baseline  
**Project:** Student Finance Manager  
**Currency:** South African Rand (ZAR)

---

# 1. Project Overview

Student Finance Manager is a full-stack web application designed to help individuals manage their personal finances through a centralised platform.

The application will allow users to record income and expenses, organise transactions into categories, establish budgets, monitor spending habits, analyse financial activity and track savings goals.

Users will be able to enter transactions manually or import transaction data using CSV bank statements.

The application will initially operate using South African Rand (ZAR) and will be accessible through desktop and mobile web browsers.

# 2. Problem Statement

Managing personal finances can be challenging when individuals lack a centralised system for tracking income, expenses, budgets and savings.

Financial information may be spread across bank statements, spreadsheets, banking applications and manually maintained records.

This makes it difficult to understand spending patterns, monitor financial goals and make informed financial decisions.

Student Finance Manager aims to address this problem by providing an accessible, structured and user-friendly platform for personal financial management.

# 3. Project Objectives

The primary objectives are to:

1. Provide a centralised platform for managing personal financial information.
2. Allow users to record and categorise income and expenses.
3. Help users establish and monitor budgets.
4. Provide meaningful financial summaries and visual analytics.
5. Allow users to establish and track savings goals.
6. Simplify transaction entry through CSV bank statement imports.
7. Protect users' personal financial information.
8. Provide a responsive experience across desktop and mobile devices.

# 4. Target Audience

The application is intended for anyone who wants to manage their personal finances.

Potential users include:
- University and college students.
- Employed individuals.
- Self-employed individuals.
- Individuals managing household finances.
- Anyone interested in monitoring their spending and savings.

The application will not be restricted to a particular occupation, age group or income level.

# 5. Functional Requirements

Functional requirements describe what the system must do.

| ID | Requirement | Description |
|---|---|---|
| FR01 | User authentication | Users must be able to register, log in and log out. |
| FR02 | Dashboard | Users must be able to view an overview of their financial position. |
| FR03 | Income management | Users must be able to create, view, edit and delete income transactions. |
| FR04 | Expense management | Users must be able to create, view, edit and delete expense transactions. |
| FR05 | Transaction categorisation | Users must be able to organise transactions into categories. |
| FR06 | Budget management | Users must be able to create, view, edit and delete budgets. |
| FR07 | Budget monitoring | Users must be able to monitor spending against budget limits. |
| FR08 | Financial analytics | Users must be able to view financial summaries, trends and charts. |
| FR09 | Savings goals | Users must be able to create and monitor savings goals. |
| FR10 | CSV imports | Users must be able to import transactions from supported CSV bank statements. |
| FR11 | Transaction filtering | Users must be able to filter transactions by relevant attributes such as date, category and transaction type. |
| FR12 | Responsive interface | The application must support desktop and mobile web browsers. |

# 6. Non-Functional Requirements

Non-functional requirements describe how the system should operate.

| ID | Requirement | Description |
|---|---|---|
| NFR01 | Security | Passwords must be securely hashed and authentication must be protected. |
| NFR02 | Data privacy | Users must only be able to access their own financial records. |
| NFR03 | Accuracy | Financial calculations must be accurate and avoid floating-point precision errors. |
| NFR04 | Performance | Common operations should execute efficiently. |
| NFR05 | Usability | The interface should be intuitive and easy to navigate. |
| NFR06 | Maintainability | The codebase must be modular, readable and maintainable. |
| NFR07 | Reliability | The application must handle invalid input and operational errors safely. |
| NFR08 | Responsiveness | The interface must adapt to different screen sizes. |
| NFR09 | Testability | Important application functionality must be covered by automated tests. |
| NFR10 | Scalability | The architecture should allow additional functionality without requiring a complete redesign. |

# 7. Version 1 Scope

## 7.1 Included Features

Version 1 will include:

- User registration and authentication.
- Personal financial dashboard.
- Income and expense management.
- Transaction categories.
- Monthly budget management.
- Budget monitoring.
- Financial analytics and charts.
- Savings goal management.
- CSV bank statement imports.
- Transaction filtering.
- Responsive desktop and mobile web interface.

## 7.2 Excluded Features

The following features are explicitly outside the scope of Version 1:

- Direct integration with banks.
- Automatic bank account synchronisation.
- Making payments or transferring money.
- AI-generated financial advice.
- Multi-currency support.
- Native Android and iOS applications.

These may be considered for future versions.

# 8. Business Rules and Initial Design Constraints

1. All financial records must belong to an authenticated user.
2. Users must not be able to access another user's financial records.
3. The initial supported currency will be ZAR.
4. Financial amounts must be stored as integer cents to prevent floating-point precision problems.
5. Transactions must be classified as either income or expenses.
6. Transactions must have valid dates and amounts.
7. Budget calculations must reflect the relevant transactions and budget period.
8. Imported CSV data must be validated before being committed to the database.
9. The system should identify potential duplicate transactions during CSV imports.
10. Users must not be required to provide online banking credentials.

# 9. Initial Technical Constraints

The proposed technology stack is:

| Component | Technology |
|---|---|
| Frontend | Angular |
| Programming language | TypeScript |
| Backend | Node.js and Express |
| Database | MongoDB |
| Database modelling | Mongoose |
| Styling | Tailwind CSS |
| Charts | Chart.js |
| Authentication | JWT and bcrypt |
| Testing | Jest, Supertest and Angular testing tools |
| Version control | Git and GitHub |
| Deployment | Vercel and Render, or suitable alternatives |

The application will initially follow a modular monolithic architecture.

Technology and architectural decisions will be documented and reviewed as development progresses.

# 10. Project Success Criteria

Version 1 will be considered successful when:

1. A user can register, authenticate and securely access their account.
2. A user can manage their own income and expenses.
3. Transactions can be organised into categories.
4. A user can create budgets and monitor spending.
5. The dashboard displays accurate financial information.
6. Financial analytics correctly represent transaction data.
7. A user can create and track savings goals.
8. Supported CSV bank statements can be imported safely.
9. The application works across desktop and mobile browsers.
10. Important functionality passes automated tests.
11. The application is deployed and accessible.
12. The project has professional technical documentation, including a comprehensive README.

# 11. Assumptions and Future Considerations

The following assumptions will guide initial development:

- The initial deployment and financial examples will use ZAR.
- CSV import support will initially target selected South African bank statement formats.
- Users will enter their transactions manually or import them through CSV files.
- The application will not connect directly to users' bank accounts.
- Each user's financial information will remain isolated from other users.

Future versions may investigate additional currencies, more bank statement formats, direct bank integrations and mobile applications.

